      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Appropriate sample pans (e.g., standard or hermetic aluminum pans)",
        "Cooling system (e.g., refrigerated cooling system) if the Tg is near or below room temperature"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ21",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Dynamic Mechanical Analysis (DMA) / Rheometry",
      "why": "This rheometer is equipped with an Environmental Test Chamber for temperature control and can measure the glass transition mechanically by tracking changes in viscoelastic moduli (storage/loss modulus and tan delta) over a temperature ramp.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Solid sample fixtures (e.g., torsion rectangular or tension geometry)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To determine the glass transition temperature (Tg) of your amorphous solid dispersion, Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) is the most common and direct approach. The DSC250 (EQ18) will measure the thermal transition as a change in heat capacity. Alternatively, you can use the HR20 hybrid rheometer (EQ21) to measure the transition mechanically, which is often more sensitive to subtle glass transitions, provided the correct solid sample fixtures are available."
}

